        "content": "\\boxed{C}.\n\nThe correct option is: C. This year most of the donations that came to Smithtown University from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university's fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors. This supports the argument by indicating that the fund-raisers' high success rate (80%) must have come from contacting new donors. However, achieving such a high success rate with new donors suggests they only targeted the easiest prospects, failing to sufficiently expand their efforts to less-likely candidates, thus supporting the claim of insufficient canvassing. \n\nThe correct option is: C. \n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**  \nThe argument posits that a high success rate (80%) indicates insufficient canvassing because fund-raisers likely focused on past donors (high-likelihood prospects) instead of expanding to new ones. Option C strengthens this by revealing that most past donors donated *without* being contacted. This means the fund-raisers\u2019 80% success rate *must* derive from new donors they contacted. However, achieving 80% success with new donors is implausibly high unless they only targeted the easiest new prospects (e.g., those almost certain to donate). This directly aligns with the argument\u2019s critique: the high success rate reflects a lack of effort to reach a broader, less-likely base of new donors. \n\nOptions A and B weaken the argument by emphasizing new donor contributions, while D neutralizes the critique by suggesting Smithtown\u2019s new donor outreach was comparable to others. Only C provides direct evidence that the fund-raisers\u2019 high success rate stems from limited, selective outreach, reinforcing the argument. \n\n\\boxed{C}",
