nd F are in different departments, which is allowed. Then D3 needs two more people. We have M left. So D3 could be P, M, and someone else. But who? We need two people. If M is in D3 with, say, G? But G is in D2. Wait, G can be in multiple departments. So D3: P, M, G. But then G is in D2 and D3. F is in D1 with H. I is in D2 with P and G. H is in D1. M is in D3 with G and P. Now check constraints:\n\n- F and G: Not together anywhere. F in D1, G in D2 and D3 \u2192 okay.\n\n- H and I: H in D1, I in D2 \u2192 okay.\n\n- F and M: F in D1, M in D3 \u2192 okay.\n\nAssignments:\n\nF: D1 (1)\n\nG: D2 and D3 (2)\n\nH: D1 (1)\n\nI: D2 (1)\n\nM: D3 (1)\n\nTotal: 6 assignments. Valid.\n\nIn this scenario, F is in D1 with H, and I is in D2. So F and I are not in the same department. Therefore, option B (F and I in the same department) is false here. So option B is not necessarily correct.\n\nNow, option C: I and P are in the same department. Since P is in all three departments, any department that I is in will include P. So I must be in a department with P. Therefore, option C states \"I and P are in charge of the same department\", which is necessarily true. Because if I is in any department, it's with P. Since I must be in at least one department, I is in a department with P. Therefore, option C must be true.\n\nWait, this is a key point. Since P is in all three departments, any assistant who is in any department must be in a department with P. Therefore, every assistant is in at least one department with P. So I must be in at least one department, and that department includes P. Therefore, option C: I and P are in the same department must be true.\n\nBut let's verify. Suppose I is in a department, but since every department has P, then I must be in a department with P. Therefore, option C is necessarily correct.\n\nIn the first scenario I considered, I was in D1 with P and F. In another scenario, I was in D2 with P and G. So in all valid scenarios, I must be in a department with P. Hence, option C is necessarily correct.\n\nWait, but the options are:\n\nC) I and p are in charge of the same department.\n\nYes. Therefore, option C must be correct.\n\nBut why did I initially think option A or B? Because I overlooked that P is in all departments. Therefore, any department that I is in must include P. Therefore, option C is always true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.\n\nLet me confirm this. Since I must be in at least one department, and every department includes P, then I and P must be in the same department. Therefore, option C must be true. So regardless of how the assignments are made, I has to be in a department with P. Therefore, the correct answer is C.\n\nIn previous scenarios where I was in D1 or D2, he was with P. So option C is always true. So the answer is C.\n\nBut wait, the options are:\n\nC) I and p are in charge of the same department.\n\nYes. So this must be true.\n\nThe other options are not necessarily true. For example, in the scenario where F is in D1 with H and P, and I is in D2 with P and G, then:\n\nA) F and H are in the same department \u2192 True in that case.\n\nBut in another scenario where F is in D1 with I and P, and H is in D3 with M and P, then A is false. Therefore, A is not necessarily correct.\n\nSimilarly, B can be false.\n\nD) M and G are in the same department. In some scenarios, like D2 having G and M, D3 having M and H, so M and G are together. But in another scenario where M is in D3 with someone else, like M in D3 with H and P, and G in D2 with I and P, then M and G are not in the same department. So D is not necessarily true.\n\nTherefore, only option C must be true.\n\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
